# Caddy on the workload host: terminates :443 so app-server's Caddy (the
# `/api/webhooks*` route added at flip time — see the runbook's Bring-up
# section) has a real TLS endpoint to proxy to. The gateway process
# (`packages/workload`) binds `127.0.0.1:8080` only — it never listens on a
# public interface itself.
#
# The site address MUST be a real hostname, not a bare `:443` — Caddy's
# `tls internal` issues a cert for whatever name(s) the site block is
# addressed by, and app-server dials this host by name
# (`workload-host.secondlayer.tools`, its SNI), then verifies the
# certificate's name matches. A bare `:443` block has no name to put on the
# cert, so app-server's TLS handshake fails the hostname check even with the
# right CA trusted (review fix B). `WORKLOAD_HOST_NAME` lets a local/manual
# test point this at something else (see the runbook's local proof).
#
# `tls internal` — Caddy's own local CA, not a public CA (ACME/Let's
# Encrypt needs a public DNS name that actually resolves to this host over
# the open internet for the HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN challenge; this host is
# firewalled to app-server's IP only on :443, so no CA could ever validate
# it that way). app-server's Caddyfile pins this CA's root explicitly
# (`tls_trust_pool file`) rather than trusting the system store, which would
# never trust a locally-issued cert. See the runbook's Bring-up section for
# copying the root CA to app-server, and for the DNS record this hostname
# needs (an A record is enough — it never needs to be internet-reachable,
# just resolvable from app-server).
{$WORKLOAD_HOST_NAME:workload-host.secondlayer.tools} {
	tls internal

	header -Server
	header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
